{
  "brand": "iolar",
  "template": "terminal-dark",
  "ratio": "3:4",
  "seed": 42,
  "series": "GIT UNDO: THE FIELD MANUAL",
  "date": "29/08/2026",
  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "The Three Trees",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Undo Is A Choice",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Git undo is not one command — you pick trees to move",
            "HEAD = pointer to the current commit, via a branch ref",
            "Index = the proposed next commit, binary `.git/index`",
            "Working tree = the actual files you edit on disk"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "$ cat .git/HEAD",
            "ref: refs/heads/main  # HEAD via branch",
            "$ git rev-parse HEAD",
            "a1b2c3d4  # commit it resolves to"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Index Is A Tree"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Holds the FULL next tree, not a diff",
            "`git add` writes a blob, updates the index entry",
            "Also called the staging area or the cache",
            "Worktree vs index = 'not staged for commit'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Reading git status"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Column",
            "Compares",
            "Label"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Left",
              "Index vs HEAD",
              "staged"
            ],
            [
              "Right",
              "Worktree vs Index",
              "not staged"
            ],
            [
              "Neither",
              "unknown to Git",
              "untracked"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Inspect Trees Directly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git diff --cached  # Index vs HEAD",
            "git diff           # Worktree vs Index",
            "git diff HEAD      # Worktree vs HEAD"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Snapshots, Not Deltas"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each commit = full tree + parent pointer(s)",
            "So restoring old state is a checkout, never a replay",
            "Blobs, trees, commits live in `.git/objects`",
            "Addressed by SHA-1 (SHA-256 in newer repos)",
            "A ref is a file containing one SHA"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Losing A Commit",
          "text": "The object survives; you lost only the reference to it. Until gc runs, the data is still on disk."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The One Question",
          "text": "Every undo command answers: which trees do I rewind, and do I rewrite history or append to it?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "git reset: One Command, Three Depths",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What Reset Does",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git reset [mode] <commit>` moves the current branch ref",
            "Then optionally updates the Index, then the working tree",
            "HEAD follows along because HEAD points at the branch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Depth Ladder"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Moves",
            "Changes appear"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "--soft",
              "HEAD",
              "staged"
            ],
            [
              "--mixed",
              "HEAD + Index",
              "unstaged"
            ],
            [
              "--hard",
              "HEAD + Index + WT",
              "destroyed"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Squash The Last Three"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset --soft HEAD~3",
            "git commit -m \"one clean commit\"  # 3 -> 1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Everyday Forms"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset HEAD file.txt  # unstage, keep",
            "git restore --staged file.txt # modern",
            "git reset --hard origin/main  # nuke local"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--hard origin/main` drops local commits AND local edits",
            "Reach for it only when local work is disposable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Path Form Is Different"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git reset <commit> -- <path>` never moves HEAD",
            "It only rewrites index entries for those paths",
            "`--hard` combined with paths is illegal"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Safer Middle Grounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Behaviour"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "--merge",
              "abort if local mods would be lost"
            ],
            [
              "--keep",
              "keep local mods, else refuse"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Two Standing Costs",
          "text": "Reset rewrites branch history — never reset a branch others pulled unless you agree to force-push. Cost is O(changed tree): a ref write plus a checkout, no history copy."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. --soft: Un-Commit Only",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Semantics: un-commit, keep everything ready to re-commit",
            "`git reset --soft HEAD~1` leaves the changes staged",
            "Safe by construction: nothing on disk changes",
            "Better than `--amend` when you want to re-split the change"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Soft, Mixed, Hard In Practice",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Soft Squash Workflow"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset --soft <base>  # collapse branch",
            "git commit -c ORIG_HEAD  # reuse old msg",
            "git reset --soft ORIG_HEAD  # undo that"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-c` reuses the old message and authorship metadata",
            "`ORIG_HEAD` is written by reset, merge and rebase",
            "The orphaned commit still exists — reflog has its SHA"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Two Soft Traps",
          "text": "Soft does not undo a push: history diverges and the next push is rejected non-fast-forward. Soft-resetting across a merge flattens it into one giant staged diff."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. --mixed Is The Default"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset HEAD~1  # == --mixed HEAD~1",
            "git reset         # unstage everything"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mixed = un-commit AND unstage; edits drop to the worktree",
            "Classic rescue after an over-eager `git add -A`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Where Work Dies",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--hard` is the only mode that destroys uncommitted work",
            "There is no reflog for the working tree",
            "No commit arg = discard local mods, stay on this commit",
            "Rule: commit or stash before any `--hard`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Untracked Files Survive"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git clean -nd  # preview what would die",
            "git clean -fd  # remove untracked files+dirs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. After The Damage"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Recoverable only if the content was once staged",
            "`git fsck --lost-found` surfaces dangling blobs",
            "`git reset --hard <c> -- path` is an error",
            "Use `git restore --source=<c> path` instead"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Soft, Mixed, Hard In Practice (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "A Two-Keystroke Pause",
          "text": "Run `git status` and `git stash list` before every hard reset. Never let `reset --hard` become muscle memory."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Inverse Diff, New Commit",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git revert <commit>` computes that commit's inverse diff",
            "Applies it as a NEW commit on top of the branch",
            "The original commit stays in history, untouched",
            "Message defaults to `Revert \"<subject>\"`",
            "Append-only, so it is safe on shared public branches"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Ranges And Batching"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git revert HEAD~3..HEAD  # 3 commits",
            "git revert --no-commit A B C",
            "git commit -m \"undo feature X\"  # 1 commit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Range form reverts newest first, one revert commit each",
            "`--no-commit` stages them all for a single commit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Reverting A Merge"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git revert -m 1 <merge-sha>  # keep mainline"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-m <parent-number>` is mandatory for a merge commit",
            "Parent 1 = the branch you merged into",
            "Undoes the side branch's contribution only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Merge-Revert Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Re-merging that branch later brings nothing back",
            "Git already counts the merge as done",
            "Fix: `git revert <revert-sha>` first, then re-merge"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Conflicts And Exits"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Command"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Conflict",
              "fix, `git add`, `--continue`"
            ],
            [
              "Bail out",
              "`git revert --abort`"
            ],
            [
              "Drop this one",
              "`git revert --skip`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Conflicts arise when later commits touched the same lines",
            "A revert of a revert restores the change — double negation"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Not For Secrets",
          "text": "Revert never removes content from history. The bad blob still sits in the old commit and is still fetchable by anyone."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Choosing: reset / revert / amend / restore",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Three Tools Compared",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "History",
            "Use when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "reset",
              "rewrites, ref back",
              "local, unpushed"
            ],
            [
              "revert",
              "adds, ref forward",
              "pushed, shared"
            ],
            [
              "--amend",
              "replaces tip, new SHA",
              "fix last commit"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Decision Rule",
          "text": "Did anyone else pull this? Yes, revert. No, reset. That single question settles almost every case."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Amend Is A Mini-Rewrite"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The old commit is orphaned; the new one has a different SHA",
            "Committer date is refreshed too",
            "`--no-edit` adds forgotten files without touching the message"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git commit --amend --no-edit",
            "git push --force-with-lease  # not --force"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`--force-with-lease` refuses if the remote moved since your fetch",
            "Plain `--force` does not check — it overwrites blind"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Trail And Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Angle",
            "reset",
            "revert"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Audit trail",
              "erased",
              "preserved"
            ],
            [
              "Objects made",
              "none",
              "one commit"
            ],
            [
              "Cost",
              "ref + checkout",
              "O(diff size)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Git 2.23 Split"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git switch` moves branches, `git restore` restores contents",
            "`git checkout <name>` was ambiguous: branch or path?",
            "`--` used to disambiguate — a genuine footgun",
            "Restore never moves HEAD and never touches history"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. restore Cheat Rows"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "restore <p>",
              "drop unstaged edits, from Index"
            ],
            [
              "restore --staged <p>",
              "unstage, from HEAD"
            ],
            [
              "--staged --worktree",
              "file back to HEAD state"
            ],
            [
              "--source=HEAD~2 <p>",
              "old content into worktree"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Legacy And switch"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git checkout -- <path>  # old restore",
            "git checkout <c> -- <path>  # old --source",
            "git switch -c feat  # was checkout -b",
            "git switch -        # previous branch"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Choosing: reset / revert / amend / restore (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Deleted A File?",
          "text": "`git restore <path>` brings a tracked file back; add `--staged` if the delete was staged. But `git restore .` discards every unstaged edit, with no reflog to save you."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What It Records",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every movement of HEAD and of each branch ref",
            "With a timestamp and the reason for the move",
            "Plain text at `.git/logs/HEAD`",
            "And `.git/logs/refs/heads/<branch>`, one line per move"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Reading It"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "$ git reflog",
            "abc1234 HEAD@{2}: reset: moving to HEAD~3",
            "def5678 HEAD@{3}: commit: add parser"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Addressing Positions"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Spec",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "HEAD@{n}",
              "nth-previous HEAD position"
            ],
            [
              "HEAD@{yesterday}",
              "where HEAD was then"
            ],
            [
              "HEAD@{2.hours.ago}",
              "time-based lookup"
            ],
            [
              "main@{1}",
              "where main pointed one move ago"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`main@{1}` reads a branch's own log, not HEAD's",
            "`git reflog show <branch>` scopes output to one ref"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Expiry And Limits"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Entry",
            "Life",
            "Knob"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Reachable",
              "90 days",
              "gc.reflogExpire"
            ],
            [
              "Unreachable",
              "30 days",
              "gc.reflogExpireUnreachable"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Strictly local: never pushed, never cloned",
            "A fresh clone therefore has an empty reflog",
            "Bare repos disable it (`core.logAllRefUpdates`)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Undo An Undo"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset --hard HEAD@{1}  # undo last move"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Works for anything that moved a ref: reset, rebase, merge",
            "Check the entry with `git show` before jumping"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Why The Panic Is Wrong",
          "text": "'reset --hard ate my commits' is almost always false. The commits are still there and the reflog still names them."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Recovering Lost Commits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Three-Step Rescue",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reflog       # 1. find the SHA",
            "git show <sha>   # 2. confirm it",
            "git branch rescue <sha>  # 3. park it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Look for the op just before the loss: reset, rebase, branch -D",
            "`git log <sha> -3` shows context before you commit to it",
            "`git reset --hard <sha>` only when you are certain"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Take Just One Commit"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git cherry-pick <sha>  # copy one commit here"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Deleted Branch"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Its tip SHA sits in the reflog `branch: Created from…` line",
            "Or dig it out with `git fsck`",
            "Re-create it: `git branch <name> <sha>`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. When Reflog Cannot Help"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git fsck --full --no-reflogs \\",
            "  --unreachable --lost-found"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "For a fresh clone, or entries already expired",
            "Findings land in `.git/lost-found/`",
            "Commits become refs; dangling blobs become SHA-named files",
            "Dangling blobs = content `git add`ed but never committed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The Only Path Back",
          "text": "Dangling blobs are the sole way to recover staged work destroyed by `reset --hard`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Botched Rebase"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git rebase --abort  # mid-flight",
            "git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD  # after"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. The Clock Is Running",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git gc` and `git prune` delete unreachable objects",
            "Past `gc.pruneExpire`, default two weeks",
            "Recover fast; never run `git gc --prune=now` while panicking"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Truly Unrecoverable",
          "text": "Working-tree edits that were never staged and never committed. Nothing in Git ever saw them, so nothing can bring them back."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What Stash Saves",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git stash` is an alias for `git stash push`",
            "Saves working tree plus index changes",
            "Then resets the working tree back to HEAD"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "git stash: Park Work Without Committing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Stashes Are Commits"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A commit holding the working-tree state",
            "Parents: HEAD plus a hidden index commit",
            "Stored under the ref `refs/stash`",
            "Being objects, a stash is durable — raw edits are not"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "$ git stash list",
            "stash@{0}: On main: wip auth  # newest",
            "stash@{1}: WIP on main: abc123 ..."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each new stash pushes the older ones down",
            "`git stash push -m \"wip auth\"` labels the entry",
            "Unlabeled stashes get useless auto messages"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Apply vs Pop"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "pop",
              "apply + drop entry"
            ],
            [
              "apply",
              "apply, keep entry"
            ],
            [
              "drop stash@{0}",
              "delete one entry"
            ],
            [
              "clear",
              "delete all — silent loss"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. What Gets Included"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Includes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "(none)",
              "tracked changes only"
            ],
            [
              "-u",
              "plus untracked files"
            ],
            [
              "-a",
              "plus ignored files"
            ],
            [
              "push -p",
              "interactive hunks"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`git stash push <pathspec>` stashes only those files",
            "`git stash show -p stash@{1}` prints the full diff"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Stale Or Dropped"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git stash branch fix-x stash@{0}",
            "git fsck --unreachable | grep commit",
            "git stash apply <sha>  # revive dropped"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`stash branch` rebuilds from the stash's base commit",
            "That is the clean fix for a stash that no longer applies",
            "Dropped stash commits are unreachable but still alive"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Pop Can Conflict",
          "text": "A conflicting pop stages the conflicts and does NOT drop the entry. Resolve, `git add`, then drop it yourself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Danger Zones And Exact Fixes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Golden Rule",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never rewrite history others have based work on",
            "Public branch, revert. Private branch, reset or rebase.",
            "Hosts enforce it: protected branches reject force-pushes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Force, Carefully"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git push --force-with-lease",
            "git push --force-with-lease=main:<sha>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The lease compares the remote ref to your remote-tracking ref",
            "A bare `git fetch` updates that ref and silently defeats it",
            "Pin the expected SHA when the branch matters"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Undo Merge Or Pull"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Situation",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Merge, unpushed",
              "`git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD`"
            ],
            [
              "Merge, pushed",
              "`git revert -m 1 <sha>`"
            ],
            [
              "Pull, match upstream",
              "`git reset --hard @{u}`"
            ],
            [
              "Pull, go back",
              "`git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Secrets And Debris"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Committed a secret? Revert is not enough — the blob stays",
            "Rewrite with `git filter-repo` or BFG, then force-push",
            "Rotate the credential; assume it is already compromised",
            "`git clean -fdx` also kills ignored files: `.env`, build caches",
            "Always dry-run with `-n` first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Detached HEAD, Submodules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Commits made detached are orphaned when you switch away",
            "Git warns and prints the SHA — `git branch <name> <sha>` keeps it",
            "Submodules and worktrees do not follow a parent reset",
            "Run `git submodule update --init --recursive` after"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Mistake, Then Fix",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Staged by mistake",
              "`git restore --staged <f>`"
            ],
            [
              "Throw away one file's edits",
              "`git restore <f>`"
            ],
            [
              "Committed too early",
              "`git reset --soft HEAD~1`"
            ],
            [
              "Bad message on the tip",
              "`git commit --amend`"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Danger Zones And Exact Fixes (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Huge file in the tip",
              "amend it out"
            ],
            [
              "Huge file, deeper",
              "`filter-repo` + force-push"
            ],
            [
              "Reset too far, --hard",
              "reflog, `reset --hard HEAD@{1}`"
            ],
            [
              "Bad merge, already pushed",
              "`git revert -m 1 <sha>`"
            ],
            [
              "Rebase went sideways",
              "`--abort`, or `reset --hard ORIG_HEAD`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Fix"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Pop hit conflicts",
              "resolve, `add`, `git stash drop`"
            ],
            [
              "`stash clear` regret",
              "`fsck --unreachable`, `stash apply`"
            ],
            [
              "Branch deleted with -D",
              "reflog tip, `git branch <n> <sha>`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "git reset --soft HEAD~1  # wrong branch",
            "git stash",
            "git switch right-branch",
            "git stash pop && git commit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. One Question Decides",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Where does the change live right now?",
            "Unstaged, staged, committed-local, or committed-and-pushed",
            "That answer picks the command; nothing else matters"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Four Answers"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "State",
            "Command"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Unstaged",
              "`git restore <path>`"
            ],
            [
              "Staged",
              "`git restore --staged <path>`"
            ],
            [
              "Committed local",
              "`git reset`"
            ],
            [
              "Pushed",
              "`git revert`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Depth Table"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Trees moved"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "soft",
              "HEAD"
            ],
            [
              "mixed",
              "HEAD + Index"
            ],
            [
              "hard",
              "HEAD + Index + Worktree"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Safety Ranking"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "revert",
              "restore --staged",
              "reset --soft",
              "reset --mixed",
              "restore",
              "reset --hard",
              "clean -fdx"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "\"I lost my commits with reset --hard\" is almost always false — the commits are still sitting in your reflog. This field manual walks every undo Git gives you: the three trees underneath them all, reset's three depths, revert for anything already pushed, restore, stash, and fsck rescues for work you thought was gone. Save it for the day your stomach drops.",
